Those corrected values follow from one Analemma length per inner circumference, not two. The sphere and tube radii remain equal.

## 2. The equal-area curve's pear-shaped lobes

The plotted reference does show an asymmetry. There's a reason for it in the parameterization, not merely in the viewer.

The current curve uses:

\[
\beta(t)=k\sin(t)\cos^2(t),\qquad \phi(t)=2t
\]

Its latitude excursion and longitude progression are not distributed symmetrically along each lobe. In addition, the reference has a tangential meeting at the west equator. That meeting can produce the visual impression of a little kink or jog even though the parameterized curve itself is continuous.

I would preserve the existing equal-area curve as the reference, but investigate a smoother alternative separately. We should not sacrifice the equal-area condition just to improve its silhouette.

