Let's not guess when we can look things up. Below is a link to the Wikipedia page on Viviani's curve.&#x20;

I imagine this curve *must* have a standard name; it's a special curve in that it crosses two antipodes, and for that reason, and the following reason, it's exactly the kind of curve geometers would certainly have studied.&#x20;

One thing I can tell you... it cannot be the intersection of a cylinder because since we've got it crossing the sphere at two antipodes, the profile of a regular cylinder would intersect the entire equator in a circular intersection. So, the cylinder has to have an ovoid cross section. So, it's a relative, but it's in a different class.&#x20;


[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viviani%27s_curve#Equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viviani%27s_curve#Equations)
@Web search

Yes, the canonical Viviani's curve intersects two antipodal points, but those are the only two it can intersect because they lie on the same axis, and that axis is parallel to the intersecting cylinder. No other antipodal intersections are possible so long as the crossing point of the curve lies on the equator of the sphere; if that is the case, as with our curve, then the only case where it croses antipodes is the case of the antipodes that are exactly 90 deg away from the equator; i.e. the north and south pole. And no, a cylender cannot cross antipodes that lie on a great circle lying 90 degrees from its long axis unless the cylender is exactly 1/2 the sphere's diameter unless the crossing point of the figure 8 leaves the surface of the sphere. In order to have the tangential contact required by Viviani's curve, and also a contact with the antipode of that point, the cylinder *must* be exactly the same circumference of the circle, in which case, the intersection is precisely a great circle, not Viviani's curve (unless we say a geodesic is a special case of Viviani's curve). Regardless, our curve that kisses at the antipode of its crossing point, the cylender cannot be circular. The only way to do it is to make it a cylendroid. Which, I imagine, is part of why your original plot had elbows.&#x20;

@Web search

[https://www.wolframalpha.com/input?i=parametric+space+curves&assumption=%22ClashPrefs%22+-%3E+%7B%22SpaceCurveClass%22%2C+%22Parametric%22%7D](https://www.wolframalpha.com/input?i=parametric+space+curves\&assumption=%22ClashPrefs%22+-%3E+%7B%22SpaceCurveClass%22%2C+%22Parametric%22%7D)
[https://www.wolframalpha.com/input?i=Viviani+curve](https://www.wolframalpha.com/input?i=Viviani+curve)
[https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EllipticCylinder.html](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EllipticCylinder.html)

@Web search

An ordinary elliptic cylinder has an elliptical directrix. But an ellipse cannot pass through three distinct collinear points \\(O,E,W\\), because a line intersects a nondegenerate ellipse in at most two points.

No, the cylendroid we're looking for does not need to pass through O; in fact, it cannot if it is to loop around the poles. It only needs to have an intersection that 1) is continuous on the surface of the cylender (which means also on the sphere), and 2) intersects both E and W.&#x20;

As a result, the cross section must have a semimajor axis that is equal to the radius of the circle, and a semiminor axis that is less than the radius of the circle. And all such cylendroids will satisfy our tangent points, and our curve. So, let's go with 2R and 1/2R

