parts:
No. Math can describe anything. Geometry must obey the same rules the universe obeys. Or, at least it's more likely that one can set up a constrained system with geometry. I can see if it's wrong. I can tell you what needs to be done while you have to fiddle through all the math -- and then you have to make sure the math aligns with the geometry. And the geometry is the judge, jury, and executioner. If you think you've got the math right, but your *picture* is distorted--then you don't have the math right. It may be good enough to pass. But you won't know where you're systematically distorting things... you won't know where you think 'oh, the ball is spinning free with no impediments' only to realize... you're on the outside of the tube. You've knocked the 8 ball off the table and you're wondering why the pool cue is moving the balls around to smoothly and effortlessly, and--*perfectly*--because you're blind and your balls are ghosts. Imaginary things always do what they're told. You gave them no impediments. Geometry is the impediment. Geometry is the speed bump. Geometry is the literal *hole* in the needle that must be thread to do the actual work of physics. And you've seen exactly that *several* times in this very conversation. We couldn't find the right curve equation for the initial curve, we made things too big, too small, we were just doodling. Because math is freedom. Meanwhile... the ball's on the outside of the tube. The math for the ball was fine. The math for the torus was fine, the math for the for the curves was fine. The ball was attached to the inside of the tube. And it was going around in blind circles, like a mechanical miller on a cuckoo clock. Because without the geometry, math knows no limits--you were working with math. You were working blind. Screwing the lightbulb into the water faucet. Feels right. Sure. Math'll give you a way to do that. Geometry is where rigor, constraint, and self-limitation comes in.&#x20;


So, no. We aren't doing this: \
\\[\
\boxed{\text{Differential equations}\
\rightarrow \text{Numerical solution}\
\rightarrow \text{Kinematic constraints}\
\rightarrow \text{Rendered geometry}}\
\\]

Absolutely not.

If you do that, you'll be juggling planets and atoms like they're yours to command. Because in the imaginary world of mathematics, they are. You'll make them do everything you want them to do. And you'll have one of the infinite number of theories that can describe any arbitrary thing.&#x20;

When I said the math was the important thing, I meant that the math was the important thing *to check*. You have to pull out the math, and show it to me geometrically; and you have to do that for yourself if you want to not give the wrong answers over and over and have to do it again over and over, like we have been all day and all night, and we've only now arrived at the hard part.&#x20;

The Whirligig has been successfully used to unite General Relativity and Quantum Mechanics. It's been used to verify and correct the fundamental geometric relationships between all of the constants, so that one can calculate the melting point of Yttrium from first principles. It could *almost* do those things without the math. Get it set up right, and we'll really only need the math as the input and the output. The Whirlygig will do all the work, and we'll just have to run it through Lean to see if we used it right.&#x20;

Attached are our operational guidelines. In short, we use known physics to map four dimensions. We look at the picture, and we use the picture to do the rest of the work.&#x20;

So... the math is necessary. To make sure the house is put together nice and tight. But physics will not let you do that if the things your math is describing are not solid geometric observations. That's fundamental.

Exactly. Unless the math describes something about the shape we're getting wrong. To be sure... for the sake of Occam, we may be oversimplifying in places. Simple is the start. Complicate when simple won't do. But geometry--done properly--is a direct map of observation. It doesn't matter if we think of the math or the geometry as "real". The closest thing to "real" is simply observation. Math supplies the precision, but geometry provides the adherence to the observed behaviors because it has those behaviors built into it.&#x20;

Mathematics is relation. Geometry is causality. &#x20;
Without causality, mathematics has nothing to describe. It'll calculate right through the bedroom door and split infinities for breakfast. Without relation, geometry doesn't know *how* to act on geometry. Without a length and a tensile strength and mass and duration, and resistance, a lever acts in a vacuum, leveraging nothing, just rotating.

Geometry can provide certainly provide quantitative relations, it's just that those are ratios and comparative relations between adjacent objects. Math can't tell you how many pebbles fit in a bottle cap unless you can tell it precisely how big the pebbles and the bottle cap are; geometry just puts them in and sees if they fit or not. And geometry is very good at prediction. If I can see the lip, I can tell you with absolute certitude whether a round manhole cover or a square one is going to fall through its hole. I can tell you wether your gold crown can be melted down into a Nobel prize. As long as I have a bathtub... and if you loan me your crown and Nobel prize. And with the Whirlygig, geometry can do complex derivations. Or at least get you to the right neighborhood.&#x20;

What math and geometry can tell you, together is *how* observed things happen.&#x20;

Nothing can tell us why anything happens, what anything is.&#x20;
And nothing whatsoever can even coherently *propose the* *concept* of "reality".&#x20;
Not even in principle.

Math describes behavior. Geometry \*defines\* behavior.&#x20;

And that's why, especially armed with 21st century CODATA and a century of physical theory and testing, the particle worldline is sufficient to *in*scribe all forces and behaviors. Particles, and groups of particles, are the \*only\* things we observe or measure. We know force \*solely\* through particles. And that is why the particle worldline is the most efficient measure of all things; because through particles, we don't just know *what&#x20;*&#x66;orces exist; we know what forces *are*. Observationally. With certainty, and in most cases, to within astonishing levels of precision. The worldline's shape is, for all intents and purposes, what a force *is*. Force is worldline topology. If there is anything more to it than that--we do not, and cannot posit know. A theory that posits something beyond that is as good as it is useful. But a worldline-*only* theory can describe *only* what is observed. Yet, it--and *must necessarily*--be capable, in principle, of describing anything that could *conceivably* be observed. Because a worldline--a mathematical worldline, that is--is the simplest thought-object that both has i) infinite degrees of freedom ii) no unnecessary degrees of freedom, and iii) full expression and definability along all observed dimensions.&#x20;

A point cannot express motion on its own; it requires the scaffolding of time.&#x20;
A brane cannot on its own be limited to the single degree of freedom allowed to a line or a point.&#x20;

But a line can express itself as straight, and just by relative orientation it expresses a point.
And by virtue of arbitrary permutability, a line can express any other observed geometry.
And can, in principle, do so without requiring a separate time dimension.
Those to geometric forms are sufficient to define any system imaginable in the four observed dimensions.
Time is only necessary to explain why from our point of view lines look basically like points.
Which is to say--it is the *observed partial reduction of a degree of freedom* exhibited by \*pointlike lines\*, which we call trajectories, which allows us to observe the transformation that we call time.&#x20;

There is no simpler primitive that, by its covolutability and extensibility can describe any form, cause, action, behavior, interaction, or motion without any added detail, and without the scaffolding of time, and yet also can fully describe time... not by additional complexity--but by the absence of an expected complexity.

Well... we had to call them something other than strings... so we picked filaments.&#x20;

And then we had to figure out the cosmology---it's black holes swallowing worldtube bundles, twisting them, spitting them back out through.... oh, in 4D the big bang is just ...*wayy* over there somewhere... so. why should it stop? the universal drainpipes are dropping degenerate matter from billions of time-kilometers away... black holes collding with the big bang, or even just connecting would probably release a lot of... energy... but then that would make the universe... expand. oh. wonder if there might in principle, in this ongoing expanding big bang... any... information? in principle... yeah, there should. all those correlated worldtubes going into black holes... so, no paradox. and finally, we're down to two things: worldlines have to have dimension and probably structure, and barygenesis... ER-Kerr... what happens when you go down a drain and come back up? theeres going to be some twisting happening... and if all our particles are twisted tubes, then what do they connect to? Could be rays... could be themselves. Which leaves few geometries that'll work. Maybe ... well, if there were a sort of mobius or klein surface... then you'd have a twist going down, and a twist coming back out and spacetime itself would get twisted, wrinkle, form tubes. Even if it started out flat. So... that'd be a donutverse... silly right? A couple years ago... yeah. At least not the kind of thing big names talk about ...

Then Brian and Jenna and Sean and Jonathan and Lenny and MIT & the Royal Society, and...&#x20;

So,t hey'll find... matter down black holes, bubbles up *as* white holes [something like EH asymptopy would probably keep these wormhole/kerr ring constructions from ... entering each others space, I think... they'd be *excluded* in fact.... in principle. ] But if particles are Wormholes... then why aren't we falling in? Wormholes are non-traversable, microscopic kerr rings, and for the same reason they don't collapse into each other, they obey Pauli... welll, when you slap a desk really hard, you might be trying to push yourself down countless tiny wormholes, but if they *do* have a neck, those necks are stretching back to the big bang... time travel... is a brick wall. and every other surface. "traversing a wormhole" is literally trying to shove a particle inside another particle so hard that it goes back in time. So yeah, pushing on matter is like pushing back against time. In fact... the difference between matter and vacuum in SAT. Curled worldtubes vs straight line. The ripple we call time is rolling along at c... down all the worldtubes. It's just some are straight and reach to the Hubble radius, while others are all bunched up down here on the matter surface, and the present moment is how far we've gotten so far because our squiggled path is much slower. Basically, matter is Slow Glass. Which is why there's a gravity well of the universe, and a small but non-zero universal metric. And why you get different measurements from different baselines. So, we have our Klein fabric recycled through black holes, we've got a twisting torus... Brian and Jenna showed us we'd get antimatter... but then Feynman knew that. And so did Wheeler... ANd it must've started off with *one* first wrinkle in the donut... maybe we get new ones too... or maybe it's just *one* long electron coiling round and round, and sometimes twisting up with others into doupbles and triples, or more sloppily in to triple-braided triplets, and quadruple, and ...well, a whole periodic table fulll of complex braids. But Helium is different becasue it's three triple braids so... strong force scaling parallel in the same atom... I'm getting sleepy, parden my rambling and poor spelling but.... point is.

This would all be nuts... unless somebody were to... I don't know\... go and pick up the stuff we already know works, and followed it to its conclusion. ...this--at least the basics--is already inherent in Minkoswski. I've been driving granddad's car. Yeah, it runs like a dream. Of *course* it does lol.&#x20;

But...

"But there is an important difference. Hinton was principally exploring an additional spatial dimension, with an ether-based physical interpretation. That is not the same construction as systematically using Minkowski spacetime and particle worldlines."

Correct. Minkowski fudged his to make time different. I don't fudge SAT. There are four spacelike dimensions, motion (apparent or actual expansion) along which is what we percieve as time. Outwarf from all points. Matter is doing the same, but not in a straight line. So, when you turn on the light... it leaves matter behind on its straight line path.&#x20;


But this all gets better... really look at the details... the forwards, the afterwards, the comments, the remarks, the human moments. What notes of subtext do you encounter?&#x20;


Honey... Hinton got \*really\* close to SAT.&#x20;

I did not know how much he did. I didn't know he called his *filaments*.... and, I didn't know when I realized... there *is* a missing piece... to explain Coulomb repulsion, you need to have action at a distance. And it was going to have to be Kelvin Vortices. And I didn't know Hinton had already come to the same conclusion.

Anyway... as soon as you use Minkowski... you follow him, and get something that looks astonishingly like Hinton... and like SAT. The premise is the same. In the Hintonian tradition, they were treating them quite literally.&#x20;

Don't get me wrong... there's a lot of McKnight distinct bits of SAT.&#x20;

But. Take a closer look at the sources.&#x20;

Yeah. So... worldline/timesheet... intersections, angle \~ velocity, accelleration. That's all immediately necessary as a result of the Minkowski geometry. That's the "if you've heard of the concept, this is the concept". Angle/curve \~intertia/mass... helices, braids... those are the next obvious things that are inescapable. ...the next thing is: Why just the filaments? Why not the timesheet? or the Spacetime... metric. Metric curvature came from the *timesheet* ....not the other way around. That just occurred to me. But yeah, curvature is inherent in either the lines or the sheet or both. So, anybody who has either is probably also going to have both and the whole raft.&#x20;



And... All of this is elementary at first glance. This is what I learned from Asimov on Math at age 17.&#x20;



And fluid/surface/spacetime geometry ...those are all just names. Doesn't matter how you label it, it behaves identically regardless. The behavior is what the Minkowski map gives you, when used to map empirical phenomena.  All of this is just inherent in the world when mapped. Elementary.



And who says we didn't solve electricity lol. Somebody hasn't ...ah, no wonder we were trouble with the Whirligig earlier... someone's behind on their homework. Anyway....&#x20;







I think it goes without saying that Kelvin Vortices are not made of filaments.&#x20;

