My understanding is that Einstein didn't treat the metric as a physical medium, but simply as geometry. But that may be a later reinterpretation.







\>He argues that it is futile to ask whether microscopic phenomena resemble an undefined kind of motion. First we must construct the possible mechanics of four-dimensional matter, then compare its consequences with observation.



No, it's just common sense and being a good empiricist.&#x20;



No, we've got plenty pf calculations. I'm having you read the history for a reason. Look for the interviewer talking about Einstein and the 4th dimension.

Oh, no, no you're right... We don't have the full Kelvin vortex calculation yet. That's new--I don't think we've done that yet; unless an early version resembled the same equations... but we've had various ways of handling various pieces over time. Our medium is at present, considered to be likely a BEC vacuum worldtube ocean, maybe something a bit different. How different a vacuum worldline is from a matter filament ...not sure. Maybe it's just a superfluid or a fluid, or maybe a membrane-like structure is enough.&#x20;

So... let's have you look at the historical books... actually read them. And other contemporary-ish books and see what the intellectual environment was, how researchers considered one another, and try to get a sense of the state of the field mainstream physics and just orient ourselves with the lay of the land and get a feel for the different periods. Don't skip materials that aren't obvously related, we want to get out the thermometer and vasoline and take the temperature of the body.&#x20;

