[$github](app://connector_76869538009648d5b282a4bb21c3d157)

Yeah, the BEC is a working hypothesis, simply the most obvious H(s)H successor to the SAT filament block universe (note: that's always been intended as a true block universe only methodologically--the original June 2025 Big Paper gives the background on these 'interpretive modes'). SAT/H(s)H has tier-based acceptance criteria. In short. Tier 1: IF it's not suggested by, or preferably required by, or one of a small number of possible choices constrained by the geometry, it's not a candidate. If so, it can be suggested. The thing is... geometry is it's a visual mathematical system with built-in 'laws' that equations don't necessarily have. Tier 2: IF it passes the first tier, and offers a neat explanation of a phenomenon with multiple intuitive interaction surfaces, it can be a candidate for one of the tentative SAT or H(s)H hypotheses. Tier 3: IF it passes the first two tiers, it remains tentative indefinitely; if and when it unexpectedly turns out, without being asked to do so, but purely as a consequence of its presence, to explain otherwise unrelated things to what inspired it--preferably in multiple completely different sectors--then it'll probably become the "official" SAT/H(s)H working hypothesis. Tier 4: IF removing it or changing it breaks or precisely distorts all of the other numbers in the model by a predictable amount, then it's part of the model.&#x20;

Also, as an aside, because this is the directive I'm giving everyone else at the moment.&#x20;

[$github](app://connector_76869538009648d5b282a4bb21c3d157)  One non-negotiable: Nothing gets into Factory floor unless it has a precise geometric plot of the system under study, checked for fidelity by hostile review, and displayed with the file. Everything in H(s)H is geometry, and if the geometry isn't correct, then the math is uncoupled, unnecessary, or irrelevant. Same goes for basically all papers. If a statement makes a point about a geometric form (and...that means basically everything we do)... then what exactly it means has to be shown.  Math allows precision and fitting. But it's not the object. It's not the physical object, and it's not the theoretical object. The theoretical object is the geometry. If we have only the math, we have the handbook to the car. That's great, but it's only a description of a model, it's a pentary object. The quaternary object is a precise static diagram. The tertiary object is the precision dynamic geometry as a software model. The secondary object is a physical working orrery or hands-on demonstration. The primary object is observation. SAT is our K-T Boundary, as it were.

We know it's not views. It's obviously *entierly* automated. But that could be entirely automated, or it could be individuals or groups with automated tools.&#x20;
I keep wondering if we're doing something that's causing clone events in our workflow. [$github](app://connector_76869538009648d5b282a4bb21c3d157)&#x20;

It's hard to say if activity drives interest or activity *is* the activity. But I'd think the number of unique cloners amongst our research would not change over time since we've had exactly five recurrance slots for some time now.&#x20;

One thought is--training data. Companies do multi-agent nibbles rather than big data scrapes sometimes. The original podcast audience was LLM cognitive research and philosophy... it seems plausible percolation through the tech/philosophy of science crowd into niche SAT-adjacent theory territory, into broader physics is a likely diffusion model approximation.

